'''Pornography''' (colloquially called '''porn''', or '''porno''') is [[sexually suggestive]] material, such as pictures, videos, text, or audio intended for [[sexual arousal]].{{efn|name="definition"}} Over the course of human history, pornographic depictions have taken many forms, from stone carvings in the [[Upper Paleolithic]], to [[Virtual reality pornography|virtual reality experiences]] in the present day. It is almost always made for [[Age appropriateness|consumption by adults]]. A distinction is often made between pornography and [[erotica]], although some viewers may not distinguish between them.

Human fascination with sexual imagery has been a constant [[History of erotic depictions|throughout history]]. The oldest artifacts considered pornographic were discovered in Germany in 2008 and are dated to be at least 35,000 years old.{{efn|name="oldest"}} However, the reception of such imagery has varied hugely by cultural context. The Indian [[Sanskrit]] text ''[[Kama Sutra]]'' (3rd century CE) contained prose, poetry, and illustrations regarding sexual behavior, and the book was celebrated; while the British English text ''[[Fanny Hill]]'' (1748), considered "the first original English [[prose]] pornography," has been one of the most prosecuted and banned books. In 1896, a film by [[Thomas Edison]] that [[The Kiss (1896 film)|depicted a kiss]] was denounced as obscene in the United States, whereas [[Eugène Pirou]]'s film ''[[Le Coucher de la Mariée|Bedtime for the Bride]]'' from the same year was received very favorably in France.

Starting from the mid-20th century, societal attitudes towards sexuality became more lenient in the [[Western world]] where legal definitions of obscenity were made limited. In 1969, ''[[Blue Movie]]'' by [[Andy Warhol]] became the first film to depict [[unsimulated sex]] that received a wide theatrical release in the United States. This was followed by the "[[Golden Age of Porn]]" (1969–1984). The introduction of [[home video]] and the [[World Wide Web]] in the late 20th century led to global growth in the pornography business. Beginning in the 21st century, greater access to the [[Internet pornography|Internet]] and affordable [[Mobile porn|smartphone]]s made pornography more mainstream.

Pornography has been vouched to provision a safe outlet for sexual desires that may not be satisfied within relationships and be a facilitator of sexual fulfillment in people who do not have a partner. Pornography consumption is found to induce psychological moods and emotions similar to those evoked during [[sexual intercourse]] and [[casual sex]]. Pornography usage is considered a widespread recreational activity in-line with other digital activities such as social media or video games.{{efn|name="use"}} People who regard porn as [[sex education]] material were identified as more likely not to use [[Condom|condoms]] in their own [[sex life]], thereby assuming a higher risk of contracting [[sexually transmitted infection]]s (STIs); performers working for [[List of pornographic film studios|pornographic studios]] usually undergo regular testing for STIs, unlike much of the general public. Comparative studies indicate higher tolerance and consumption of pornography among adults tends to be associated with their greater support for [[gender equality]]. Among [[Feminist movements and ideologies#Groupings|feminist groups]], some seek to abolish pornography, believing it to be harmful, while others oppose censorship efforts, insisting it is benign. A [[longitudinal study]] ascertained pornography use is not a predictive factor in [[intimate partner violence]].{{efn|name="ipviolence"}} ''[[Porn Studies]]'', started in 2014, is the first international [[peer-reviewed]], [[academic journal]] dedicated to critical study of pornographic products and services.

Pornography is a major influencer of people's perception of sex in the [[digital age]]; a few pornographic websites rank among the [[List of most-visited websites|top 50 most-visited websites]] worldwide. Called an "erotic engine", pornography has been noted for its key role in the development of various communication and media processing technologies. For being an early adopter of innovations and a provider of [[financial capital]], the pornography industry has been cited to be a contributing factor in the adoption and popularization of new media-related technologies. The exact economic size of the porn industry in the early 21st century is unknown, although in 2023 estimates of the total market value stood at over US$172&nbsp;billion. The legality of pornography varies across countries. From the mid-2010s, unscrupulous pornography, such as [[deepfake pornography]] and [[revenge porn]] have become issues of concern.

==Etymology and definition==
{{Main|Definitions of pornography}}
The word ''pornography'' is a conglomerate of two ancient Greek words: {{lang|grc|[[:wikt:πόρνος|πόρνος]]}} ({{lang|grc-latn|pórnos}}) "fornicators",
and {{lang|grc|[[:wikt:γράφειν|γράφειν]]}} ({{lang|grc-latn|gráphein}}) "writing, recording, or description".{{sfn|McNair|2013|p=20}} In Greek language, the term ''pornography'' connotes depiction of sexual activity;{{sfn|etymonline|2023}} no date is known for the first use of the term ''pornography'', the earliest attested, most related word found is {{lang|grc|[[:wikt:πορνογράφος|πορνογράφος]]}} ({{Lang|grc-latn|pornographos}}) i.e. "someone writing about harlots" in the 3rd century CE work ''[[Deipnosophists]]'' by [[Athenaeus]].{{sfn|Liddell|Scott|1940}}{{sfn|Athenaeus|1927}}

The oldest published reference to the word ''pornography'' as in 'new pornographie,' is dated back to 1638 and is credited to [[Nathaniel Butter]] in a history of the Fleet newspaper industry.{{sfn|McNair|2013|p=21}} The modern word ''pornography'' entered the English language as the more familiar word in 1842 via French "{{Lang|fr|pornographie}}," from Greek "{{Lang|el-latn|pornographos}}".{{sfn|etymonline|2022}}

The term ''porn'' is an abbreviation of pornography.{{sfn|etymonline|2023}} The related term {{lang|grc|[[:wikt:πόρνη|πόρνη]]}} ({{Lang|grc-latn|pórnē}}) "prostitute" in [[Greek language|Greek]], originally meant "bought, purchased" similar to {{Lang|grc-latn|pernanai}} "to sell", from the [[proto-Indo-European]] root ''per-'', "to hand over" — alluding to act of selling.{{sfn|etymonline|2023}}

The word ''pornography'' was originally used by [[classical scholars]] as "a bookish, and therefore inoffensive term for writing about prostitutes",{{sfn|Talvacchia|2010}} but its meaning was quickly expanded to include all forms of "objectionable or obscene material in art and literature".{{sfn|Talvacchia|2010}} In 1864, ''[[Webster's Dictionary]]'' published "a licentious painting" as the meaning for ''pornography'',{{sfn|Talvacchia|2010}} and the ''[[Oxford English Dictionary]]'': "obscene painting" (1842), "description of obscene matters, obscene publication" (1977 or earlier).{{sfn|OED|1989}}

Definitions for the term "pornography" are varied, with people from both pro- and anti-pornography groups defining it either favorably or unfavorably, thus making any definition very [[Stipulative definition|stipulative]].{{sfn|McKee|Byron|Litsou|Ingham|2020}}{{sfn|Graves|2015}}{{sfn|Mikkola|2019|pp=2–12}} Nevertheless, academic researchers have defined pornography as sexual subject material such as a picture, video, text, or audio that is primarily intended to assist [[sexual arousal]] in the consumer, and is created and commercialized with "the consent of all persons involved".{{efn|Pornography can be defined as "material [e.g., pictures, films, videos or text] deemed sexual, given the context, that has the primary intention of sexually arousing the consumer, and is produced and distributed with the consent of all persons involved" (McDonald & Kirkman, 2019, p. 163). Central in the definition of pornography is the consent of all persons involved. Therefore, materials that were produced or distributed without the consent of at least one person involved (e.g., "revenge porn", "child pornography") were excluded from this definition (McDonald & Kirkman, 2019).{{sfn|Komlenac|Hochleitner|2022}} Pornography is best defined as a medium, such as a picture, video, or text, that is intended to be treated as sexually arousing (Rea, [41]). [...] pornography is framed as an aid for sexual arousal (Parvez, [32]).{{sfn|Speed|MacDonald|Parks|Doucette|2021}} Using pornography means to intentionally look at, watch, read, or listen to sexually arousing material (pictures, videos, films, written text or audio) which depicts nudity or explicit sexual behavior. This does not include participating in interactive sexual experiences in person or online, such as a "lap-dance" in a strip club or a live sex chat.{{sfn|Lawless|Karantzas|Knox|2023}}|name="definition"}} Arousal is considered the primary objective, the ''raison d'etre'' a material must fulfill for it to be treated as pornographic.{{sfn|McNair|2013|p=19}} As some people can feel aroused by an image that is not meant for sexual arousal and conversely cannot feel aroused by material that is clearly intended for arousal, the material that can be considered as pornography becomes subjective.{{sfn|McNair|2013|p=19}}

==History==
{{See also|History of human sexuality}}

===Prehistoric times===
Pornography is viewed by historians as a complex cultural formation.{{sfn|Lubey|2022|p=31}} Depictions of a sexual nature existed since [[prehistoric]] times as seen in [[Venus figurine]]s and [[rock art]].{{sfn|Rudgley|2000|pp=184–200}} People across various civilizations created works that depicted explicit sex; these include artifacts, music, poetry, and murals among other things that are often intertwined with religious and supernatural themes.{{sfn|Tarrant|2016|p=11}} The oldest artifacts, including the [[Venus of Hohle Fels]], which is considered to be borderline pornographic, were discovered in 2008 CE at a cave near Stuttgart in Germany, [[radiocarbon dating]] suggests they are at least 35,000 years old, from the [[Aurignacian]] period.{{efn|Anthropologist Paul Mellars of Stony Brook University in New York state says the focus on exaggerated sexual features fits with other artifacts found from the period, including phalluses carved out of bison horn and vulva inscribed on rocks. "It's sexually exaggerated to the point of being pornographic", Mellars says. "There's all this sexual symbolism bubbling up in that period. They were sex-mad." Conard used radiocarbon dates from bones and other artifacts found nearby to date the figurine. "It's at least 35,000 calendar years old, but I think it's much older than that", Conard says.{{sfn|Science|13 May 2009}}|name="oldest"}}

[[File:Venus vom Hohle Fels-01.JPG|thumb|upright|The [[Venus of Hohle Fels]] may be an ancient example of pornographic work. Standing at approximately six centimeters tall and carved from mammoth ivory, the artifact is estimated to be at least 35,000 years old.{{efn|name="oldest"}}]]

===Ancient and medieval times===
Vast number of artifacts discovered in ancient [[Mesopotamia]] region had explicit depictions of heterosexual sex.{{sfn|Black|Green|1992|pp=150–152}}{{sfn|Nemet-Nejat|1998|p=137}} Glyptic art from the [[Sumer]]ian [[Early Dynastic Period (Mesopotamia)|Early Dynastic Period]] frequently showed scenes of frontal sex in the missionary position.{{sfn|Black|Green|1992|pp=150–152}} In Mesopotamian [[Ex-voto|votive plaques]] from the early second millennium ({{circa|2000|1500{{nbsp}}BCE}}), a man is usually [[Doggy style#History and etymology|shown]] penetrating a woman from behind while she bends over drinking beer through a straw.{{sfn|Black|Green|1992|pp=150–152}} [[Assyria|Middle Assyrian]] lead votive [[figurine]]s often [[Missionary position#History|portrayed]] a man standing and penetrating a woman as she rests on an altar.{{sfn|Black|Green|1992|pp=150–152}} Scholars have traditionally interpreted all these depictions as scenes of [[hieros gamos]] (an ancient sacred marriage between a god and a goddess), but they are more likely to be associated with [[Inanna]], the Mesopotamian goddess of sex and [[sacred prostitution]].{{sfn|Black|Green|1992|pp=150–152}} Many sexually explicit images, including models of male and female sexual organs were found in the temple of Inanna at [[Assur]].{{sfn|Black|Green|1992|pp=150–152}}

Depictions of sexual intercourse were not part of the general repertory of ancient Egyptian formal art, but rudimentary sketches of heterosexual intercourse have been found on pottery fragments and in graffiti.{{sfn|Robins|1993|pp=189–190}} The final two thirds of the [[Turin Erotic Papyrus]] (Papyrus 55001), an Egyptian papyrus scroll discovered at [[Deir el-Medina]],{{sfn|Robins|1993|pp=189–190}} consists of a series of twelve [[Vignette (literature)|vignette]]s showing men and women in various [[sexual position]]s. The scroll was probably painted in the Ramesside period (1292–1075 BCE) and its high artistic quality indicates that it was produced for a wealthy audience. No other similar scrolls have yet been discovered.{{sfn|Robins|1993|pp=189–190}}

