===Economics===
The production and [[distribution (business)|distribution]] of pornography are economic activities of some importance. In Europe, [[Budapest]] is regarded as the industry center.{{sfn|Bridge|1996}}{{sfn|Zook|2005}} Other pornography production centers in the world are located in Florida (US), Brazil, Czech Republic, and Japan.{{sfn|Goldstein|Steinberg|Aynalem|Kerndt|2011}} In the United States, the pornography industry employs about 20,000 people including 2,000 to 3,000 performers,{{sfn|Tarrant|2016|p=49}} and is centered in the [[San Fernando Valley]] of Los Angeles. By 2005, it became the largest pornography production center in the world.{{sfn|Rodriguez-Hart|Chitale|Rigg|Goldstein|2012}}{{sfn|Goldstein|Steinberg|Aynalem|Kerndt|2011}} Apart from regular media coverage, the industry in the US receives considerable attention from private organizations, government agencies, and political organizations.{{sfn|AVN|15 July 1999}}

[[File:Porn DVD and books vending machines in a Japanese backwoods area.jpg|thumb|upright=1.1|A [[vending machine]] in Japan selling adult entertainment products. In 2018, [[pornography in Japan]] was estimated to be worth over $20&nbsp;billion.{{sfn|Strusiewicz|2018}}{{sfn|Hambleton|2019|p=361}}]]

As of 2011, pornography was becoming one of the biggest businesses in the United States.{{sfn|D'Orlando|2011}} In 2014, the porn industry was believed to bring in at least $13&nbsp;billion on a yearly basis in the United States.{{sfn|Szymanski|Stewart-Richardson|2014}} Through the 2010s, many pornography production companies and top pornographic websites such as [[Pornhub]], [[RedTube]] and [[YouPorn]] have been acquired by [[MindGeek]], a company that has been described as "a monopoly" in the pornography business.{{sfn|Auerbach|2014}} This development was identified as a problem. According to Marina Adshade, a professor from the [[Vancouver School of Economics]] and the author of ''Dollars and Sex: How economics influences sex and love'', having a monopoly in the pornography business has forced the producers to reduce their charges, and radically changed the work of performers "who are now under greater pressure to perform acts that they would have been able to refuse in the past", all at a lower price without profits for themselves.{{sfn|Harford|2019}} Some pornographic productions are often linked to [[prostitution]].<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Tyler |first1=Meagan |title=Harms of production: Theorising pornography as a form of prostitution |journal=Women's Studies International Forum |date=2015 |volume=48 |pages=114–123 |doi=10.1016/j.wsif.2014.11.014 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Donevan |first1=Meghan |last2=Jonsson |first2=Linda S. |last3=Svedin |first3=Carl Göran |title=The experience of individuals filmed for pornography production: A history of continuous polyvictimization and ongoing mental health challenges |journal=Nordic Journal of Psychiatry |date=2025 |volume=79 |issue=2 |pages=156–165 |doi=10.1080/08039488.2025.2464634 |pmid=39955768 }}</ref><ref>Melissa Farley, Erica Bergkvist et al.: ''[https://www.prostitutionresearch.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/PornographyProduction-HarmSweden-1.pdf Pornography Production Harm in Sweden: Filmed Prostitution is Inseparable from Non-Filmed Prostitution] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250123102636/https://prostitutionresearch.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/PornographyProduction-HarmSweden-1.pdf |date=23 January 2025 }}''. October 17, 2023.</ref>

Online pornography is available both for a fee and free of charge.{{sfn|Döring|2009}} The availability of free porn on the Internet has led to a decline in the business of mainstream pornography.{{sfn|Kopp|2020|p=11}}{{sfn|Harford|2019}} Piracy is estimated to result in losses of some $2&nbsp;billion a year for the porn industry.{{sfn|Macmillen|2017}} Budgets of many studios reduced considerably and contracts for performers became less common.{{sfn|Macmillen|2017}} Reportedly, applications by established [[List of pornography companies|pornography companies]] for porn-shoot permits in [[Los Angeles County]] fell by 95 percent during the period 2012 to 2015.{{sfn|Kopp|2020|p=11}}{{sfn|Forrester|2016}} According to Mark Spiegler, an adult talent agent, in the early 2000s female performers made about $100,000 a year. By 2017, the amount is about $50,000.{{sfn|Macmillen|2017}}

The technological era led to decline of the studio and "the rise of the pornography worker herself".{{sfn|Lubey|2022|p=215}} Newer ways of monetization have opened for the pornography workers who are taking the path of entrepreneurship.{{sfn|Lubey|2022|p=215}} In 1995, [[Jenna Jameson]] signed her first contract with the porn studio [[Wicked Pictures]].{{sfn|Forrester|2016}} After building a brand image for herself she started her own company [[ClubJenna]], which by 2005 was reportedly earning an annual revenue of $30-$35&nbsp;million.{{sfn|Forrester|2016}}{{sfn|Macmillen|2017}} "Performers are hustlers now," said [[Chanel Preston]] (a performer who was also chairperson of the [[Adult Performer Advocacy Committee]]), while noting that performers have to be creative to sustain their income and reach audience, both of which, she said are mainly achieved through "feature dancing, selling merchandise, webcamming", among other activities.{{sfn|Macmillen|2017}} "Custom" pornography made according to the requests of customer clients has emerged as one new business niche.{{sfn|Harford|2019}} The average career for the new age performer lasts about four to six months.{{sfn|Forrester|2016}} Before moving on to the business side, adult performers use studio works to advertise and build a brand image for themselves. They acquire an audience who would later pay at personal website or [[Webcam model|webcam performances]].{{sfn|Lubey|2022|p=215}}{{sfn|Macmillen|2017}} Commercial webcamming, which emerged in the 1990s{{sfn|Weitzer|2023|p=195}} as a niche sector in the adult entertainment industry, grew to become a multibillion-dollar business by the mid-2020s.{{sfn|Weitzer|2023|p=14}}

The exact economic size of the porn industry in the early-twenty-first century is unknown to anyone.{{sfn|Rosen|2023}} Kassia Wosick, a sociologist from New Mexico State University, estimated the global porn market value at $97&nbsp;billion in 2015, with the US revenue estimated at $10 and $12&nbsp;billion. IBISWorld, a leading researcher of various markets and industries, calculated total US revenue to reach $3.3&nbsp;billion by 2020.{{sfn|Rosen|2023}}{{sfn|NBCnews|20 January 2015}} On the basis of a research report by a market analysis firm, ''[[USA Today]]'' published that the estimated worth of the adult entertainment industry market in 2023 is over $172&nbsp;billion.{{sfn|Austin|2024}}

===Technology===
Pornographers have taken advantage of each major technological advancement for the production and distribution of their services.{{sfn|Barss|2010|p=3}} Pornography has been called an "erotic engine" and a driving force in the development of various media related technologies from the [[printing press]], through photography (still and motion), to [[satellite TV]], [[Home video]], and [[streaming media]].{{sfn|Barss|2010|p=1,10}}

One of the world's leading anti-pornography campaigners, [[Gail Dines]], has stated that "the demand for porn has driven the development of core cross-platform technologies for [[data compression]], [[Search engine|search]], [[Data communication|transmission]] and [[micro-payments]]."{{sfn|McNair|2013|p=23}} Many of the technological developments that had been led by pornography have benefited other fields of human activity too.{{sfn|McNair|2013|p=23}} In the early 2000s, [[Wicked Pictures]] pushed for the adoption of the [[MPEG-4]] file format ahead of others, this later became the most commonly used format across high-speed Internet connections.{{sfn|Nowak|2011|p=210}} In 2009, [[Pink Visual]] became one of the first companies to license and produce content with a software introduced by a small Toronto-based company called "Spatial view", which later made it possible to view [[3D computer graphics|3D content]] on [[iPhone]]s.{{sfn|Nowak|2011|p=14}}

As an early adopter of innovations, the pornography industry has been cited to be a crucial factor in the development and popularization of various media processing and communication technologies.{{sfn|Nowak|2011|p=14}}{{sfn|McNair|2013|p=10}} From innovative smaller film cameras, to the [[VCR]]s, and the Internet, the porn industry has employed newer technologies much ahead than other commercial industries, this early adoption provided the developers their early [[financial capital]], which aided in the further development of these technologies.{{sfn|Nowak|2011|p=4}}{{sfn|Barss|2010|p=2}} The success of innovative technologies is predicted by their greater use in the porn industry.{{sfn|AVN|11 March 2014}}
