}}

====Rise of feminist pornography====
The [[lesbian feminist]] movement of the 1980s is considered a seminal moment for the women in porn industry as more women entered into the developmental side, allowing women to gear porn more towards women as they knew what women wanted, both from the perspective of actresses as well as the female audience. This involved making [[lesbian pornography]] that is not merely geared towards heterosexual males—a change considered good, as for a long time the porn industry had been directed by men for men.{{sfn|Ziv|2014}}

Furthermore, the advent of the [[VCR]], [[home video]], and affordable [[video cameras]] allowed for the possibility of [[feminist pornography]].{{sfn|Comella|Erickson|2013|pp=79–96}} Feminist porn directors are interested in challenging representations of men and women, as well as in providing sexually-empowering imagery that features many kinds of bodies.{{sfn|Comella|Erickson|2013|pp=320–328}} [[Angela White]] started her own production company, AWG Entertainment, in which she has complete creative control over the content—from her partners, to the location, costumes, and the "vibe" of the video, "I am a feminist, so what I create is feminist, and I produce ethical porn, which is when everything is consensual", she said.{{sfn|Topsfield|2022}}

Women are more likely to consume porn that is "female-centered" and feature acts such as [[cunnilingus]]. Female-centric porn is mostly made by women, in these works the initiation of sexual activity is done by the female.{{sfn|Salmon|Fisher|Burch|2020|p=554}} [[Porn for women]] is identified by factors like greater attention on "sensual surroundings" and "soft focus camerawork", rather than on explicit depiction of sexual activity, making the productions more warm and humane compared to the traditional porn made for heterosexual men.{{sfn|McNair|2013|p=18}}
<blockquote>
"If feminists define pornography, per se, as the enemy, the result will be to make a lot of women ashamed of their sexual feelings and afraid to be honest about them. And the last thing women need is more sexual shame, guilt, and hypocrisy—this time served up by feminism" — [[Ellen Willis]].{{sfn|Hester|2014|p=24}}</blockquote>

====Pay rates====
Porn industry has been noted for being one of the few industries where women enjoy a power advantage in the workplace. Female performers can often dictate which male actors they will and will not work with. Porn is one of the few contemporary occupations where the [[Gender pay gap|pay gap]] operates in the favor of women. The average actress makes fifty to a hundred per cent more money than her male counterpart.{{sfn|Faludi|1995|pp=65–66}}{{sfn|Morris|2016}}{{sfn|Topsfield|2022}}

=== Psychological perspective ===
[[Psychologist]]s consider pornography to be of particular relevance in the study of [[intimate relationship]]s and the development of [[adolescent sexuality]].{{sfn|Grubbs|Kraus|2021}} Mainstream [[psychology]] is mostly concerned with the study of [[effects of pornography]], while [[critical psychology]] and [[applied psychology]] is engaged in more nuanced and academic study of pornography.{{sfn|Barker|2014}} Problematic pornography use is assessed in [[clinical psychology]].{{sfn|Grubbs|Kraus|2021}}

A 2013 study refuted the notion that porn actresses have higher rates of psychological problems than regular women. The study compared 177 porn actresses with regular women of similar age, ethnicity, and marital status, and found that the porn actresses had "higher levels of self-esteem, positive feelings, social support, sexual satisfaction, and spirituality" compared to the regular women.{{sfn|Griffith|Mitchell|Hart|Adams|2013|pp=621–632}}

==== Psychoanalyst views ====
In [[analytical psychology]], humans sexual and [[List of religions and spiritual traditions|religious-spiritual]] instincts are considered tightly associated with each other, with both sharing a common objective, which, as [[Carl Jung]] acknowledged, is the striving of the [[Psyche (psychology)|psyche]] for "wholeness".{{sfn|Tricarico|2018|p=61}} The psyche of a person is understood to be differentiated, being made-up of [[Trait theory|traits]] that are [[feminine]] and [[masculine]] in nature.{{sfn|Tricarico|2018|p=68}} According to Jung, this differentiation allowed the formation of opposite polarities that make "[[consciousness]] possible".{{sfn|Pettis|2014|p=62}} According to psychologist and author Giorgio Tricarico, as an individual moves through various life experiences, their psyche approaches wholeness or the state of "[[Nondualism|non-differentiated]]"—a realm of higher nondual consciousness—considered belonging to the sacred or divine.{{sfn|Tricarico|2018|pp=57–78}} In [[Tantra#Hindu tantra|Hindu tantric]] view, the guiding image of a male and a female conjoined in sexual intercourse represent the embodiment of nondual consciousness.{{sfn|Madden|2014|pp=1777–1779}} Men and women, however they appear, are considered microcosmic compounds of the macrocosmic principles—[[Shiva]] (matter) and [[Shakti]] (spirit).{{sfn|Camphausen|1999|p=225}} Shiva and Shakti, together in "perpetual union", form the nondual "[[Absolute (philosophy)|Absolute]]".{{Sfnmp|Jain|2014|1pp=14-15|Leeming|2014|2pp=1646-1647|Sarbacker|2011|3pp=34, 38}}

[[File:India, Himachal Pradesh, Mandi, 19th century - Kali on Shiva - 2014.650 - Cleveland Museum of Art.tif|thumb|Shiva and Shakti in a state of "copulation" represent the flow of "erotic energy" in an individual.{{sfn|Jain|2014|pp=14–15}} Painting showing goddess [[Kali]] (a personification of Shakti) in union with [[Shiva]]—together ''it'' forms the [[Existence|self-existent]] nondual Absolute. 19th century portrait, in [[Cleveland Museum of Art]], U.S.]]
[[Sigmund Freud]] called the feminine Shakti "[[libido]] that cannot be simply repressed."{{sfn|Bailey|2014|p=1669}} Self-realization or becoming aware of the " 'deep' femininity" entails dealing with the powerful sexual energy.{{sfn|Plancke|2020}} Tantric rituals like [[maithuna]] harness the sexual energy in order to make the male and the female principles that appear seemingly opposite-arrive into unity or a harmonious whole in the "divine feminine" or "unified divine consciousness", an idea analogous with analytical psychology's ''[[coincidentia oppositorum]]''.{{sfn|Madden|2014|pp=1777–1779}} In classical Hindu thought, the nature of the [[Ātman (Hinduism)|self]] in males and females is assessed as being [[Androgyny|androgyne]], with sexuality being a creative function of the divine to align the human self with its bipolar nature.<ref>{{multiref |1={{harvnb|Narayanan|2008|p=569}} |2={{harvnb|Lidke|2003|p=125}} |3={{harvnb|Saran|2018|pp=10, 45}}}}</ref> The masculine and the feminine principles of the self were identified with Shiva and Shakti, who make-up the two [[Sexual polarity|sexual polarities]]; by establishing a connection between the two, for the flow of erotic energy (as in an [[Electric current#Conventions|electric circuit]] between positive and negative terminals for the flow of [[electric current]]), in one's own being—by the means of sexual stimulation—through "erotic visualization" or "ritual copulation", the self would "divest" from its body identity and realign into the "[[Ardhanarishvara|bipolar being]]", which, then represents a unit microcosm mirroring the nondual macrocosm; thus an individual in [[Brahman#Advaita Vedanta|being one]] with the absolute experiences ''bliss''-considered as the power of the goddess (Shakti) in a tangible form.<ref>{{multiref |1={{harvnb|Lochtefeld|2002|pp=[https://archive.org/details/TheIllustratedEncyclopediaOfHinduism.../page/n700/mode/1up?view=theater 665-667]}} |2={{harvnb|Octopus|1975|pp=122–124}} |3={{harvnb|Saran|2018|p=180}}}}</ref>

In the Hindu tantric view, the women who participate in union rituals, thereby enabling men to attain self-realization are regarded as shakti or the goddess, as they are believed to embody ''her''.{{sfn|Camphausen|1999|p=205}} Recognition of the deity in an objective woman is centered upon a man's acceptance of the ''[[Subject and object (philosophy)|subjective]]'' feminine and the primacy of her desires.{{sfn|Biernacki|2006|p=200}} Tricarico professed that modern-day pornography in its essence is a "desacralised, technological, and consumerist" equivalent of the ancient [[sacred prostitution]] {{endash}} a custom that involved honoring of the sacred feminine and worship of the prostitutes as [[goddess]].{{sfn|Tricarico|2018|pp=70–71}} The feminine is believed to embody particular qualities of the sacred or divine more broadly and deeply than the masculine, consequently in women, the ability to incorporate [[Nondualism|nondualistic]] awareness is assumed to be higher.{{sfn|Tricarico|2018|p=68}} Tricarico argued that women in porn, through their performances of many sexual acts, would inadvertently approach the non-differentiated state, an effect which he called the "intimation of [[hierophany]]".{{sfn|Tricarico|2018|p=68}} "Porn actresses may embody the medium to enter what used to be the realm of the sacred", he said.{{sfn|Tricarico|2018|p=77}} The actresses have been likened to the "descendants of the lost goddesses" who are now offering the gift of the "[[numinous]]" to all through their performances, but are unacknowledged or devalued for their contributions.{{sfn|Tricarico|2018|p=77}}

=== Religious attitudes ===
{{Main|Religious views on pornography}}

Many [[religions]] have long and vehemently opposed a wide range of sexual behaviors, as a result religious people are found highly susceptible to experience great distress in their use of pornography. Religious people who use pornography tend to feel sexually ashamed.{{sfn|Volk|Thomas|Sosin|Jacob|2016|pp=244–259}} [[Sexual shame]]—which arises from a person's perception of their self in other peoples mind, and a negative assessment of their own sexuality—is considered a powerful factor that over time governs an individual's behavior.{{sfn|Volk|Thomas|Sosin|Jacob|2016|pp=244–259}}{{sfn|Gordon|2018|pp=105–123}} As sexuality is interwoven into one's personal [[Identity (social science)|identity]], sexual shame or sexual embarrassment are found to attack the person's very sense of self.{{sfn|Volk|Thomas|Sosin|Jacob|2016|pp=244–259}}

When a sexual shaming event occurs, the person attributes causation to oneself resulting in self condemnation, and experience feelings of sadness, loneliness, anger, unworthiness, and rejection, along with a perceived judgment of their self by others.{{sfn|Volk|Thomas|Sosin|Jacob|2016|pp=244–259}} In this mental landscape, a fear arises that ones sexual self needs to be hidden. This psychological process initiates and fuels further shame and lowers one's self-esteem.{{sfn|Volk|Thomas|Sosin|Jacob|2016|pp=244–259}} Sexual shame constricts the "psychic space for free play with one's sexuality".{{sfn|Cole|2014}} Sexual shame in people begets more [[shame]], and leads to a cycle of powerlessness culminating in deepening negative emotions.{{sfn|Volk|Thomas|Sosin|Jacob|2016|pp=244–259}} Those who tend to feel shame easily are found to be at greater risk for [[Depression (mood)|depression]] and [[anxiety disorders]].{{sfn|Kämmerer|2019}} According to clinical psychologist Gershen Kaufman, all [[Sexual dysfunction|Sexual disorders]] are majorly "disorders of shame".{{sfn|Kaufman|2004}}

The cause of attributing shame to sexuality is traced back to the [[biblical]] interpretation of nakedness being shameful.{{efn|In the bible, nakedness is a source of shame. The [[Book of Genesis]] 2:25 says of Adam and Eve, "And they were both naked, the man and his wife, and were not ashamed." That changed when they rebelled against God's commandment and ate of the tree of knowledge. From then on, they felt ashamed in each other's presence: "And the eyes of them both were opened, and they knew that they were naked; and they sewed fig leaves together, and made themselves aprons." This biblical interpretation of nakedness as shameful still deeply informs the social norms and conventions that determine how we deal with human physicality and sexuality. Although our notions of whether, how, where and in the presence of whom a person may be undressed have changed over the centuries, the shame we feel when we transgress the norms has remained.{{sfn|Kämmerer|2019}}}} Much of the [[Christian mythology]] presented sexuality as an obstacle to be surmounted in the way of salvation. The major [[Abrahamic religions]] condemn and consider all forms of ''nonmarital'' and ''nonreproductive'' sexual pleasure as unacceptable. In [[Hinduism]], [[bhoga]] (sexual pleasure) is celebrated as a value in itself and is considered one of the two ways to [[Nirvana#Hinduism|nirvana]], the other being the more demanding [[yoga]]. In the Hindu tantric view, watching coitus as an act of Shiva and Shakti is believed to unfurl the [[Kundalini]], and is considered equivalent to one's engaging in [[maithuna]] or the fifth M of the [[panchamakara]].{{efn|[T]he Goddess is not a static icon but an active partner with Siva in erotic love making. Watching this is so intoxicating that it befuddles the senses and opens the final door for the rising kundalinî. [...] watching Siva and Sakti in union is the equivalent of engaging in the fifth M oneself.{{sfn|McDermott|2018|pp=169–170}}}} A central concept in Hinduism, [[purushartha]], advocates pursuit of the four main goals for happiness: [[dharma]] (virtue), [[artha]] (riches), [[kama]] (pleasure), and [[moksha]] (freedom).{{sfn|Flood|1996|p=17}} The pursuit of Kama was elaborated by the sage Vatsyayana in his treatise ''[[Kama Sutra]]'', which states that sexual pleasure and food are essential for the well-being of the body, and on both of them depend virtue and prosperity.{{sfn|Vatsyayana|1994|p=[https://archive.org/details/the-complete-kama-sutra-the-first-unabridged-modern-translation-of-the-classic-i/page/42/mode/1up?view=theater 42]}}{{sfn|Ludo|1985}} Food, despite causing indigestion sometimes, would still be consumed regularly, and so it must be with pleasure, which must be pursued with caution while eliminating unwanted or harmful effects. As no one abstains from cooking food worrying about beggars who ask for it, or restrain from sowing wheat fearing animals that destroy the crop, similarly, instructs [[Vatsyayana]], that men and women acquire knowledge of Kama by the time they reach youth and pursue it even though dangers exist; and those who become accomplished in ''Dharma'', ''Artha'', and ''Kama'' would attain highest happiness in this world and hereafter.{{sfn|Vatsyayana|1994|pp=[https://archive.org/details/the-complete-kama-sutra-the-first-unabridged-modern-translation-of-the-classic-i/page/43/mode/1up?view=theater 42-43]}}{{sfn|Ludo|1985}}

According to [[the Buddha]], happiness is of two types: one derived from "domestic life" and the other from "monastic life", and between the two, monastic kind is "superior".{{sfn|Numrich|2009|p=68}} As a result of the Buddha's effective advocacy for [[monasticism]], in Buddhist communities, marriage and divorce remained civil matters and never acquired sacremental significance.{{sfn|Numrich|2009|p=69}} Counsel over [[sex life]] for householders was minimal, while for the monks it was extensive as in [[Vinaya]] since all sexual behaviors were meant to be suppressed for the sake of [[Enlightenment in Buddhism|enlightenment]].{{sfn|Numrich|2009|p=68}} The early Buddhist texts castigated women as detrimental beings. The Buddha himself said often that a woman's body is "a vessel of impurity, full of stinking filth. It is like a rotten pit ... like a toilet, with nine holes pouring all sorts of filth."{{sfn|Faure|2003|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=RvO0vO575owC&pg=PA56 56]}} Once when it came to his notice that a monk, Suddina, transgressed celibacy with his wife for the sake of progeny, the Buddha chided him saying, "It were better for you, foolish man, that your male organ should enter the mouth of a terrible and poisonous snake, than that it should enter a woman."{{sfn|Langenberg|2015|p=278}} Per the Buddha, all sexual desires are incompatible with [[Enlightenment in Buddhism|enlightenment]].{{sfn|Langenberg|2015|p=278}} In [[Buddhism]], people who even derive pleasure from watching others engage in sexual activity were relegated as [[pandaka]] (pusillanimous).{{sfn|Numrich|2009|p=70}} The Buddha said sexuality is a [[Fetter (Buddhism)|fetter]] that must be evaded completely and men who engage with it are "[[Purity in Buddhism|impure]]" and will not be freed from "old age".{{sfn|Numrich|2009|p=67}} After [[the Buddha]] died in old age,{{sfn|Cousins|1996}} subsequent generations of Buddhists resolved their problematic attitudes towards sex by accommodating different views.{{sfn|Langenberg|2015|p=278}}{{sfn|Numrich|2009|p=68}}

According to Indonesia's foremost Islamic preacher, [[Abdullah Gymnastiar|A. Gymnastiar]], shame is a noble emotion commanded in the Islamic scripture and was held high by the prophet, who had been quoted as saying "Faith is compiled of seventy branches... and shame is one of them." To cultivate shame in Muslims, their sexual gaze needs to be checked, as unchecked gaze is believed to be the door through which [[Satan]] enters and soils the heart.{{sfn|Hoesterey|2016}} In 2006, when anti-pornography protests erupted in [[Indonesia]], the world's most populous [[Islam by country|Muslim-majority country]], over the publication of the inaugural Indonesian edition of ''[[Playboy]]''{{snd}}Gymnastiar called for a legislation to ban pornography and embarked on a mission to shroud the state with a sense of shame, giving the slogan "the more shameful, the more faithful". During these protests, Indonesia's foremost Islamic newspaper, ''[[Republika (Indonesian newspaper)|Republika]]'', published daily front-page editorials which featured a logo of the word pornografi crossed out with a red X. The Jakarta office of ''[[Playboy Indonesia]]'' was ransacked by members of the Islamic Defenders Front (Front Pembela Islam or FPI), and bookstore owners were threatened not to sell any issue of the magazine. Consequently, in December 2008, Indonesian lawmakers signed an [[Pornography Law (Indonesia)|anti-pornography bill]] into law with overwhelming political support.{{sfn|Hoesterey|2016}}

Highly religious people are more likely to support policies against pornography such as censorship.{{sfn|Droubay|Butters|Shafer|2021}} Ironically, regions with highly religious and conservative people were found to search for more pornography online.{{sfn|Shultz|2014}} Religious people are prone to having obsessive thoughts regarding [[sin]] and punishment by God over their pornography use causing them to feel ashamed, and perceive themselves to have [[pornography addiction]] while also suffering from [[Sexual obsessions#Obsessive-compulsive disorder|OCD]] related symptoms.{{sfn|De Jong|Cook|2021}} A study of sexually active religious people found those who are highly spiritually matured have less shame, while those not spiritually matured have high shame.{{sfn|Keller|Mollen|Rosen|2015}}

